It only defaults when the request omitted a date, and it uses a plain `LocalDate.now()` with no weekend/business-day adjustment. The `registrationDate` field itself lives in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` (line 55, with getter/setter at 101–107).

### 3) Membership number generation (with the year segment)

The membership number is not persisted — it is computed on read by the mapper. `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`

```java
@Mapping(target = "membershipNumber", expression = "java(membershipNumber(owner))")
@Mapping(target = "membershipTier", expression = "java(membershipTier(owner))")
...
/** The owner's membership number, formatted '<customerCode>-M<YY>' where YY is the
 * last two digits of the registrationDate year. */
default String membershipNumber(Owner owner) {
    if (owner.getCustomerCode() == null || owner.getRegistrationDate() == null) {
        return null;
    }
    return String.format("%s-M%02d", owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100);
}
```

The **year segment** is `owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100` (last two digits, `-M<YY>`). The `customerCode` half of the number is assigned earlier in the pipeline by `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`:

```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
    String cityPrefix = prefix(owner.getCity());
    String lastNamePrefix = prefix(owner.getLastName());
    int sequence = OwnerCities.size(ownerRepository.findAll(), owner.getCity()) + 1;
    owner.setCustomerCode(String.format("%s-%s-%04d", cityPrefix, lastNamePrefix, sequence));
}
```

So the full membership number is `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>-M<YY>`. The OpenAPI schema documents this at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` lines 2117–2124.

### 4) Daily create-limit enforcement

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RejectDailyOwnerLimit.java`

```java
public class RejectDailyOwnerLimit {

    /** Maximum number of owners that may be registered in a single day. */
    static final int DAILY_LIMIT = 100;

    public void service(OwnerRepository ownerRepository) throws DailyOwnerLimitException {
        LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();
        int count = 0;
        for (Owner existing : ownerRepository.findAll()) {
            if (today.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate())) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        if (count >= DAILY_LIMIT) {
            throw new DailyOwnerLimitException(DAILY_LIMIT);
        }
    }
}
```

It counts owners whose stored `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` and throws once the count reaches 100. The exception maps to HTTP 429:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DailyOwnerLimitException.java` (message `"Daily limit of " + limit + " owner registrations has been reached"`)
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler.java` (responds `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`)
- wired via `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.DailyOwnerLimitException.yml`

Note: this reject step runs *before* `defaultRegistrationDate`/`save`, and it counts by "today" only. Because the limit counts already-persisted owners against `LocalDate.now()`, it lines up with how `DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate` stamps new owners.

### 5) Related tests

The only registration/membership/limit-related tests are the acceptance tests:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`

```java
/** business-day: the default registration date must fall on a business day (a weekend rolls
 * forward to Monday). Can't force the server's clock black-box, so assert the invariant that holds
 * every day: the default registrationDate is never a Saturday or Sunday. */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay() throws Exception {
		int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
		DayOfWeek dow = LocalDate.parse(fetchOwner(id).get("registrationDate").asText()).getDayOfWeek();
		assertTrue(dow != DayOfWeek.SATURDAY && dow != DayOfWeek.SUNDAY, dow.toString());
	}
}
```

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` provides the harness/helpers: `ownerNode()` (line 117, returns a fully-unique owner body), `createOwnerOk()` (line 160), `fetchOwner()` (line 200), plus a class comment (lines 37–42) noting that owner-create rules — "membership number, per-day cap, per-city cap, 'most common city'" — are exercised black-box.

## One discrepancy worth flagging

The acceptance test `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay` asserts the default `registrationDate` is **never a Saturday or Sunday** (weekends should "roll forward to Monday"). However, `DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate.service()` sets the raw `LocalDate.now()` with no weekend-rolling logic. As written, this test would fail whenever an owner is created on a weekend — the "business-day" behavior described in the test/comment is not implemented in `DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate.java`.

